<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Once upon a Time... – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Legends of Panama</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Once upon a Time...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Create a short narrative based on a picture. Interactive: Collaborate on a group project report.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a short narrative inspired by a picture of a Panamanian legend (e.g., "Once upon a time, La India Dormida watched over the valley. After the battle, the villagers remembered her sacrifice.").
            Can share ideas for a group project on legends in Panama (e.g., "Let's include the legend of El Gallo Vicente.").
            Can contribute sentences for a group report on a Panamanian legend (e.g., "Our report is about the legend of La India Dormida.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows 3-4 pictures of famous Panamanian legends (e.g., El Chivato, La Tulivieja, La India Dormida) without names. Students work in pairs to write down 3-5 words they remember from previous lessons related to these legends or stories in general (e.g., <i>legend, monster, hero, village, mountain, scary, old</i>). Teacher asks pairs to share their words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher displays a simple picture (e.g., a man walking in a forest) and models writing 2-3 sentences about it using past simple and past continuous, incorporating adverbs of time. For example: "Once upon a time, a man <b>was exploring</b> the forest. <b>Suddenly</b>, he <b>saw</b> a mysterious creature. He <b>felt</b> brave." The teacher clearly articulates words with -ed and -ing endings, emphasizing the pronunciation of /t/, /d/, and /ɪd/ in past tense verbs (e.g., *walked* /t/, *played* /d/, *waited* /ɪd/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'He <b>was exploring</b>', does it mean the action happened quickly or continued for some time?" (It continued for some time.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What words can we use to start a story about old times?" (<i>Once upon a time, Long ago</i>)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Legends of Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative excerpt (1-2 paragraphs) of a Panamanian legend, such as "El Gallo Vicente," displayed on the board or as a handout. The text will contain examples of past simple, past continuous, adverbs of time, sequencing words, and target vocabulary (e.g., <i>legend, village, brave, fight, saved, suddenly, once upon a time</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Telling a story: "Once upon a time, the hero saved the village." Sequencing events: "First, the hero saw the witch. Then, he fought with her."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the narrative individually. Then, they complete the following short writing tasks:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> Write one sentence summarizing what the legend is about.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match parts of sentences from the story (e.g., "El Gallo Vicente was protecting..." to "...the villagers at night.") on a worksheet.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identify & Write:</b> Write down all the verbs in the past simple and past continuous from the text.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Past continuous (e.g., <i>was fighting</i>), Adverbs of time (e.g., <i>yesterday, suddenly</i>), Past simple (e.g., <i>celebrated, ended</i>). Vocabulary: <i>legend, monster, hero, village, fight, save, explore, appear, brave, strong, once upon a time, after, during, in the end</i>. Pronunciation of -ed endings (/t/, /d/, /ɪd/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Gap-fill):</b> Students receive sentences related to Panamanian legends with blanks. They must write the correct past simple or past continuous form of the verb in parentheses or choose the appropriate adverb/sequence word from a box.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "Once upon a time, La India Dormida ________ (watch) over the valley. ________ (After / During) the battle, the villagers ________ (remember) her sacrifice."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled (Sentence Building):</b> The teacher provides jumbled sentence parts or keywords. Students *write* complete sentences, using correct past tenses, sequence words, and target vocabulary, to tell a logical sequence of events.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "hero / mountain / exploring / was / the / . / suddenly / creature / appeared / a" -> Students write: "The hero <b>was exploring</b> the mountain. <b>Suddenly</b>, a creature <b>appeared</b>."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Sentence Writing):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives a picture of a different simple scene related to a legend (e.g., a witch hiding, a hero running). Each student writes 2-3 descriptive sentences about their picture, using past simple and past continuous. They then exchange their written sentences with their partner, checking for correct grammar, spelling, and appropriate vocabulary. They provide constructive written feedback on their partner's sentences.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Legends of Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task:</b> Group Narrative Creation – "Our Panamanian Legend Report"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are divided into small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a unique, detailed picture depicting a scene from a Panamanian legend (e.g., La India Dormida looking over mountains, El Chivato appearing in a village, a hidden treasure in a river). Their task is to collaboratively *write* a short narrative (2-3 paragraphs) based on the picture, as if creating a 'report' about the legend.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Brainstorming:</b> Groups discuss the picture, brainstorming characters (<i>hero, monster, spirit</i>), setting (<i>village, mountain, river</i>), and possible events (<i>fight, save, appear</i>). They share ideas for their group project report, e.g., "Let's include the legend of El Gallo Vicente."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Individual Sentence Contribution:</b> Each student in the group is assigned to write 2-3 sentences that contribute to the overall narrative, focusing on different parts of the story (e.g., "Student A writes the beginning," "Student B writes the middle action," "Student C writes the ending"). They must use:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Past simple and past continuous verbs.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least one adverb of time (e.g., <i>suddenly, long ago</i>) and one sequence word (e.g., <i>after, before</i>).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least five target vocabulary words from the list provided (e.g., <i>legend, monster, hero, treasure, village, explore, appear, brave, mysterious</i>).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative Writing & Editing:</b> Groups combine their sentences to form a cohesive narrative. They read it aloud to each other, clarifying details in group discussions (Sociolinguistic Competence). They collaboratively edit the report for clarity, grammar, spelling, and flow. Each group aims to produce a written 'report' on their chosen Panamanian legend. (e.g., "Our report is about the legend of La India Dormida.").</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during group work using a checklist (e.g., "Used past simple," "Used past continuous," "Included sequence words," "Contributed to group"), and peer assessment of the final written narratives.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group submits their written narrative about the Panamanian legend.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> (e.g., <i>legend, monster, hero, village, fight, save</i>)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Correct Grammar:</b> Application of Past Simple and Past Continuous.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Narrative Structure:</b> Inclusion of adverbs of time and sequence words (e.g., <i>once upon a time, suddenly, after, in the end</i>) to logically sequence events.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Cohesion and Coherence:</b> The story flows logically and makes sense.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Evidence of group members contributing sentences and ideas.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new vocabulary word you used in your group's story today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now about writing a short story using past tenses (Past Simple and Past Continuous)?" (1-5 scale)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompts:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of writing your group's legend, and how did you overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did working with your group help you write a better story?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., specific activities, student engagement with pictures, effective use of group work).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students to improve their narrative writing skills and command of past tenses? (e.g., more examples of sequencing events, explicit instruction on paragraph structure).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students effectively clarify details in group discussions? How can this be improved?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The narratives written by the groups in this lesson will be used as the foundation for the "Mediation & Integration" lesson (Lesson 5), where students will summarize their stories for other groups and prepare a short presentation to share their legends with the class, fostering their 21st-century skills project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>